      "doc-ar-2024 page 1 of 18\n\n\n## directors' report\n\nyour directors present the annual report together with the audited financial statements for the year\n\nended 31 march 2025.\n\n\nfinancial highlights\n\n\nthe principal financial results for the year and the\n\ncomparative period is set out below, drawn from the\n\nmeasurement and billing records for the contract.": "Col1",
      "the year under review saw steady execution across the company's business units. your directors\nremain focused on disciplined bidding, timely certification of running-account bills and recovery of\nretention and statutory receivables.\n\n\nmanagement's discussion — operating review\n\n\nthe financial year under review was executed against an order book that the board considers\nadequate for the near term. revenue is recognised on the percentage-of-completion basis across the\ncontract portfolio, and the movement in turnover between the comparative periods reflects the stage\nof execution reached on the larger packages rather than any change in the commercial terms on\n\nwhich work is taken.\n\n\nmargins remained within the range the board regards as sustainable for this class of work. input costs\n— principally cement, steel and bitumen — continued to move with the published indices, and where a\ncontract carries a price-variation clause the recovery has been billed in the normal course. where it\ndoes not, the exposure was absorbed within the tendered rates.\n\n\nreceivables continue to be dominated by government and public-sector clients, whose payment cycles\nare longer than those of private counterparties but whose credit risk the board assesses as low.\nretention money is released progressively on expiry of the defect liability periods, and no material\namount is regarded as doubtful of recovery at the balance-sheet date.\n\n\nthe company met its statutory obligations in respect of goods and services tax, tax deducted at source\nand provident-fund contributions during the year, and holds current registrations for every jurisdiction\nin which it operates.\n\n\nstate of affairs & order book\n\n\n17 contracts remained in execution as at year end with an aggregate awarded value of rs. 56796.04\nlakh (including approved variations of rs. 13823.06 lakh across 75 variation orders). 7 credit notes\naggregating rs. 160.09 lakh were absorbed in revenue.\n\n\ndoc-ar-2024 page 2 of 18\n\n\n## management discussion & analysis\n\nsegment commentary\n\n\nthe segmental analysis below follows the internal work-category reporting used by management to\nallocate resources. movements between categories year on year reflect the natural completion cycle of\nthe underlying packages rather than a deliberate change in the company's market focus; a single\nlarge package reaching substantial completion can move a category materially in either direction.\n\n\nsegment revenue (net)\n\n\nthe following table records revenue by work category, net of\n\ncredit notes, reconciled against the underlying vouchers\n\nbefore issue.": "Segment",
      "quarterly net revenue\n\n\nthe table below sets out the distribution of revenue across\n\nthe four quarters, as certified by the officers named at the\n\nfoot of this document.": "Quarter",
      "client concentration\n\n\nthe board reviews client concentration at each meeting. a significant share of billings arises from a\nlimited number of public-sector authorities, which is characteristic of the sector and of the tendering\n\n\ndoc-ar-2024 page 3 of 18\n\n\nprocess through which work is secured. the board does not consider this a concentration of credit\nrisk, but it does keep the position under review and continues to tender across a spread of authorities\nand geographies.\n\n\nprincipal clients (by billings)\n\n\nthe following table records the clients accounting for the\n\nlargest share of billings, as certified by the officers named at\n\nthe foot of this document.": "Client",
      "order inflow remained healthy across roads, water and building segments. margins were protected\nthrough procurement discipline and recovery-focused billing.\n\n\ndoc-ar-2024 page 4 of 18\n\n\n## balance sheet as at 31 march 2025": "EQUITY AND LIABILITIES",
      "## statement of profit & loss for the year ended 31": "Col1",
